Acute Asthma Exacerbation
Respiratory System
Expiratory wheeze, Acute dyspnea, Reduced PEFR

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-001
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of wheeze and dyspnea failing to respond to standard reliever medication, necessitating immediate review to prevent impending respiratory failure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated increasing respiratory distress and inadequate response to initial bronchodilator therapy, requiring urgent assessment to prevent health deterioration prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My chest feels like it has a tight band around it and I cannot catch my breath.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began 12 hours ago with a dry, minimally productive cough. Over the past 4 hours, there has been an acute onset of dyspnea and widespread expiratory wheeze. The patient reports a band-like constriction around the chest and tachypnea out of proportion to exertion. There is no relief from regular salbutamol inhaler use, demonstrating an evolving pattern of acute bronchospasm with early signs of physiological stress such as tachycardia.
Functional Status: Independent but currently limiting movement due to shortness of breath.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone, high risk for delayed escalation if symptoms worsen.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 24 breaths/min | SpO2: 94% on room air | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Anxious appearance, sitting upright, moderate respiratory distress with intermittent accessory muscle use, speaking in partial sentences.
Physical Exam: Respiratory system examination reveals widespread expiratory wheeze bilaterally and a prolonged expiratory phase. Acute dyspnea is evident with accessory muscle use. Cardiovascular exam shows mild tachycardia. PEFR is reduced at 250 L/min, demonstrating reversible airway obstruction.
Environmental Observations: Evidence of dust in living areas, indoor heating on high setting.
Medication Reconciliation: Salbutamol inhaler checked; nearly empty canister noted.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Asthma Exacerbation - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of pleuritic chest pain, unilateral leg swelling, or hemoptysis.

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of crushing central chest pain radiating to the jaw or arm, and presence of widespread wheeze.

Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of high fever, productive purulent sputum, or focal lung crackles.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute exacerbation of asthma characterized by significant tachypnea, reduced PEFR, and widespread expiratory wheeze that has failed to respond to their usual beta-agonist therapy. The ongoing accessory muscle use and inability to speak in full sentences indicate moderate to severe respiratory distress. Delaying assessment and intervention until the next in-hours period would pose an unacceptable risk of further airway narrowing, potential respiratory failure, and need for emergency intubation. Therefore, an urgent after-hours home visit was clinically mandated to deliver immediate bronchodilation, oral corticosteroids, and stabilize the patient.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered nebulized salbutamol 5mg and ipratropium bromide 500mcg. Prescribed oral prednisolone 50mg daily for 5 days.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce future exacerbations, use a spacer device with the inhaler every single time; doing so ensures the medication reaches the lungs rather than the back of the throat, directly resulting in faster symptom relief and improved breathing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required at this time.
DME/Safety (Optional): New spacer device recommended.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours to assess response to oral corticosteroids.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the nearest Emergency Department or call 000 if dyspnea worsens, lips turn blue, confusion develops, or if the reliever inhaler fails to provide relief for more than 2 hours.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis is presumptive pending formal spirometry investigations; asthmatic conditions are dynamic and can change rapidly.
Clinical Course: Expected improvement in dyspnea within 24 to 48 hours following initiation of oral steroids; failure to improve necessitates immediate medical review.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical care if you experience difficulty breathing at rest, cannot speak in full sentences, or if your reliever medication provides less than 2 hours of relief.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Oral steroids are required to reduce the underlying inflammation in the airways, while the nebulizer provides rapid relaxation of the bronchial muscles.
Actionable Steps: Take the steroid tablets in the morning with food; this step establishes a routine that reduces stomach irritation and prevents sleep disturbance, leading to better medication adherence.

